Summary
The visual analysis of WallStreetBets posts from 2020–2021 highlights several key patterns in retail investor attention and discussion behavior.
First, engagement on the platform is highly uneven. The distributions of post scores and comment counts show that most posts receive limited interaction, while a small number attract extremely high attention. This indicates that retail investors tend to concentrate collectively on a limited set of highly visible discussions, suggesting that investment interest is driven by a few prominent topics rather than evenly distributed across posts.
Second, there is a strong positive relationship between post scores and comment activity. Posts that receive higher voting scores also tend to generate more discussion, demonstrating that investor attention tends to cluster: when a topic becomes popular, it attracts both visibility and active participation. This pattern reflects the collective and social nature of investment decision-making within the community.
Third, temporal patterns suggest that investor discussions are influenced by timing and external events. Engagement levels vary across hours of the day and days of the week, indicating that posting time can affect visibility and interaction. In addition, fluctuations in average engagement over the study period, particularly noticeable peaks in early 2021, align with periods of heightened retail investor activity in financial markets. This suggests that real-world market events strongly shape online discussion intensity and investment focus.
Finally, the dominance of text and image posts suggests that retail investors primarily share ideas and react to opportunities through short-form discussions and visual content, rather than long-form posts or external media. This highlights the fast-paced and conversational nature of investment communication within the WallStreetBets community.
Overall, the visual evidence indicates that retail investor attention on WallStreetBets during 2020–2021 was shaped by collective engagement dynamics, timing effects, and external market events, rather than by simple structural features of individual posts.

